<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jesuit Missions of Chiquitos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1691">1691</a>–<a href="/years/1767">1767</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> Missions of Chiquitos were a group of mission towns founded among the Chiquitano and other peoples in the lowlands of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> between 1691 and 1760. The Jesuits organised the towns around large wooden churches and taught European music and crafts. After the Jesuits were expelled in 1767 the towns survived, and six of the churches are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jesuit-missions-of-chiquitos.jpg" alt="Jesuit Missions of Chiquitos" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bamse · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Concepcion_church.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The missions lie in the hot, semiarid lowlands of the Santa Cruz department of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, east and north-east of Santa Cruz de la Sierra, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and Guapay rivers, in a region called the Chiquitania. The Spanish conquistador Ñuflo de Chavés introduced the name Chiquitos, meaning little ones, after the small doors of the straw houses of the inhabitants. The name has since been used loosely both for the largest ethnic group, correctly the Chiquitano, and for the more than 40 ethnic groups of the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> in 1568 with the permission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>. Evangelising the Guarani of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> proved difficult, so, encouraged by Agustín Gutiérrez de Arce, the governor of Santa Cruz, they turned to the Chiquitania, where Christian teaching was more readily accepted. Between 1691 and 1760 they founded eleven missions. Fires, floods, plagues, famines and conflict with hostile peoples or slave traders forced many to be refounded or rebuilt, and epidemics of European diseases killed up to 11 percent of the population in a single episode. The Jesuits aimed at a theocratic state within a state in which the native peoples would remain autonomous and apart from Spanish colonists and rule.</p><p>The reductions were self-sufficient communities of 2,000 to 4,000 inhabitants, usually headed by two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> priests together with the cabildo and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a>, who kept their functions. The Jesuits learned the local languages, and Chiquitano became the common language of the missions, uniting numerous peoples into one ethnic group. They introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> breeding, and taught European music as a first step to conversion, among them the Swiss Jesuit Martin Schmid, who directed the making of violins, harps and organs, so that choirs and orchestras played <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> works. Historians have disagreed over how far the Jesuits controlled the people, some describing the missions as jungle utopias and others as theocratic regimes of terror.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a> signed a decree in February 1767 expelling all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> from Spanish territories. Most reductions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> were abandoned and fell into ruins, but those of Chiquitos survived largely intact.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designated six former missions as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1990: San Xavier, San Rafael de Velasco, San José de Chiquitos, Concepción, San Miguel de Velasco and Santa Ana de Velasco. Their churches combine native and European building, and are mostly of wood and adobe. A restoration project began in 1972 under the former Swiss Jesuit and architect Hans Roth. The towns centre on a rectangular plaza with the church complex on one side and the houses of the inhabitants on the other three, and they are now secular municipalities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jesuit Missions of Chiquitos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mato Grosso was a remote military frontier facing the Jesuit missions of Moxos and <b>Chiquitos</b> in Spanish Charcas.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-mato-grosso">Captaincy of Mato Grosso</a></li></ul></section>
